Routine Immunisation Administration (Childhood/Adult/Travel)
General Practice Other
Scheduled preventative vaccination, Screening for contraindications, Intramuscular/subcutaneous injection, Post-vaccination observation

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-038
Reason for Visit: Acute severe panic attack and needle phobia requiring immediate medical de-escalation prior to a mandatory, time-critical occupational vaccination.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a healthcare worker who required an immediate, mandatory Hepatitis B booster tonight to commence a critical clinical placement at 0700 hours tomorrow. The patient suffers from severe, paralyzing needle phobia and experienced a full panic attack when attempting to attend a pharmacy clinic earlier today. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to provide a highly controlled, low-stress environment, administer psychiatric de-escalation, and successfully deliver the intramuscular injection to secure their occupational status. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in the immediate cancellation of their clinical placement.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I need this Hep B shot for my hospital placement tomorrow, but I am absolutely terrified of needles and I keep hyperventilating when I see one."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents for a scheduled preventative vaccination (Hepatitis B booster) required for occupational clearance. The patient is exhibiting extreme psychological distress. A thorough screening for absolute contraindications such as previous severe anaphylaxis to vaccine components is negative; the patient's prior reactions have been purely psychological (vasovagal/panic). An assessment of current wellness confirms a complete absence of acute febrile illness. The consultation requires careful discussion of potential common adverse reactions including mild localized pain, and obtaining explicit informed consent prior to administration. The patient is highly motivated but psychologically blocked by the phobia.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but currently exhibiting high psychomotor agitation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate threat to occupational advancement; extreme situational anxiety.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly shaking, tearful, looking away from the medical bag, rapid breathing.
Physical Exam: General: No signs of acute illness. Integumentary: Left deltoid exposed and intact, suitable for injection. The clinical procedure requires preparation and intramuscular injection of the specific vaccine, followed by the provision of post-vaccination observation for 15 minutes to monitor for immediate severe reactions or vasovagal syncope. Expected investigation results include the generation of a protective antibody titre on specific serological testing weeks post-administration if clinically indicated to prove occupational immunity.
Environmental Observations: Patient seated on the floor leaning against the couch (preventative positioning for vasovagal syncope).
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Routine Immunisation Administration (Complicated by Severe Needle Phobia) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

True Vaccine Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms (tachycardia, tachypnea, shaking) are occurring entirely before the administration of the vaccine, confirming a purely psychological panic response.

Acute Febrile Illness - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal temperature and absence of infectious symptoms, clearing the patient for vaccination.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense fear is strictly localized to the specific stimulus of the needle (specific phobia) rather than pervasive worry.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient required a time-critical, mandatory occupational vaccination but was incapacitated by a severe specific phobia and panic attack. Telephone triage accurately identified that the psychological barrier required specialized medical management to prevent the loss of the clinical placement. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to utilize clinical de-escalation techniques, position the patient safely on the floor to prevent injury from a potential vasovagal faint, and swiftly administer the vaccination while the patient was distracted. By completing the procedure in a private, low-pressure environment, the acute psychiatric crisis was resolved, and the patient's occupational requirements were met. Deferring this would have resulted in an unmanageable crisis and occupational failure tomorrow.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Employed extensive verbal distraction and grounding techniques. Administered the Hepatitis B vaccine (1mL IM) into the left deltoid while the patient was safely seated on the floor. Provided a mandatory post-vaccination observation for 15 minutes; no vasovagal syncope or anaphylaxis occurred. Documented the vaccine batch number and anatomical site of administration, and completed the occupational clearance paperwork.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage future needle encounters, commit to the "Applied Tension Technique" whenever you sit in the waiting room for an injection. Tense the muscles in your arms, legs, and torso for 15 seconds until your face feels warm, then release. Repeat this five times. By actively increasing your blood pressure through deliberate muscle tension, you physically prevent the sudden drop in blood pressure that causes the fainting reflex, heavily reducing the fear of passing out.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks for a quantitative Hepatitis B surface antibody titre to confirm immunity for the hospital.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you develop sudden, severe difficulty breathing, swelling of your face or lips, or a widespread, intensely itchy rash in the hours following the injection.

